1
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:06,920
I fully expected to find a Constable in the kitchen,

2
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:10,960
waiting to take me up. But not only was there no Constable there,

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:16,480
but no discovery had yet been made of the robbery. Mrs. Joe was prodigiously busy in

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:21,719
getting the house ready for the festivities of the day, and Joe had been put upon the kitchen

5
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:28,239
doorstep to keep him out of the dust-pan,—an article into which his destiny always led him,

6
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:34,480
sooner or later, when my sister was vigorously reaping the floors of her establishment.

7
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:39,280
“And where the deuce ha’ you been?” was Mrs. Joe’s Christmas salutation,

8
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:42,320
when I and my conscience showed ourselves.

9
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,960
I said I had been down to hear the Carols. “Ah! well!” observed Mrs.

10
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:52,119
Joe. “You might ha’ done worse.” Not a doubt of that I thought.

11
00:00:52,119 --> 00:00:56,920
“Perhaps if I warn’t a blacksmith’s wife, and (what’s the same thing) a slave with

12
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:02,599
her apron never off, I should have been to hear the Carols,” said Mrs. Joe. “I’m rather

13
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:08,320
partial to Carols, myself, and that’s the best of reasons for my never hearing any.”

14
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:14,199
Joe, who had ventured into the kitchen after me as the dustpan had retired before us, drew the back

15
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:20,559
of his hand across his nose with a conciliatory air, when Mrs. Joe darted a look at him, and,

16
00:01:20,559 --> 00:01:27,760
when her eyes were withdrawn, secretly crossed his two forefingers, and exhibited them to me, as our

17
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:35,000
token that Mrs. Joe was in a cross temper. This was so much her normal state, that Joe and I would

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,320
often, for weeks together, be, as to our fingers, like monumental Crusaders as to their legs.

19
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:48,239
We were to have a superb dinner, consisting of a leg of pickled pork and greens,

20
00:01:48,239 --> 00:01:54,079
and a pair of roast stuffed fowls. A handsome mince-pie had been made yesterday morning (which

21
00:01:54,079 --> 00:02:00,679
accounted for the mincemeat not being missed), and the pudding was already on the boil. These

22
00:02:00,679 --> 00:02:06,400
extensive arrangements occasioned us to be cut off unceremoniously in respect of breakfast;

23
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,679
“for I ain’t,” said Mrs. Joe,—“I ain’t a-going to have no formal cramming and

24
00:02:10,679 --> 00:02:15,880
busting and washing up now, with what I’ve got before me, I promise you!”

25
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:21,199
So, we had our slices served out, as if we were two thousand troops on a forced march instead

26
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:27,239
of a man and boy at home; and we took gulps of milk and water, with apologetic countenances,

27
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:33,559
from a jug on the dresser. In the meantime, Mrs. Joe put clean white curtains up,

28
00:02:33,559 --> 00:02:38,920
and tacked a new flowered flounce across the wide chimney to replace the old one, and uncovered

29
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:43,960
the little state parlour across the passage, which was never uncovered at any other time,

30
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:49,360
but passed the rest of the year in a cool haze of silver paper, which even extended to the four

31
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:54,599
little white crockery poodles on the mantel-shelf, each with a black nose and a basket of flowers

32
00:02:54,599 --> 00:03:01,079
in his mouth, and each the counterpart of the other. Mrs. Joe was a very clean housekeeper,

33
00:03:01,079 --> 00:03:06,760
but had an exquisite art of making her cleanliness more uncomfortable and unacceptable than dirt

34
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:15,800
itself. Cleanliness is next to Godliness, and some people do the same by their religion.

35
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:21,760
My sister, having so much to do, was going to church vicariously, that is to say,

36
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:29,639
Joe and I were going. In his working-clothes, Joe was a well-knit characteristic-looking blacksmith;

37
00:03:29,639 --> 00:03:34,559
in his holiday clothes, he was more like a scarecrow in good circumstances, than anything

38
00:03:34,559 --> 00:03:41,159
else. Nothing that he wore then fitted him or seemed to belong to him; and everything that he

39
00:03:41,159 --> 00:03:47,519
wore then grazed him. On the present festive occasion he emerged from his room, when the

40
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:55,800
blithe bells were going, the picture of misery, in a full suit of Sunday penitentials. As to me,

41
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:00,559
I think my sister must have had some general idea that I was a young offender whom an Accoucheur

42
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:06,599
Policeman had taken up (on my birthday) and delivered over to her, to be dealt with according

43
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:12,280
to the outraged majesty of the law. I was always treated as if I had insisted on being born in

44
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:18,519
opposition to the dictates of reason, religion, and morality, and against the dissuading arguments

45
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:24,280
of my best friends. Even when I was taken to have a new suit of clothes, the tailor had orders to

46
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:30,679
make them like a kind of Reformatory, and on no account to let me have the free use of my limbs.

47
00:04:30,679 --> 00:04:36,239
Joe and I going to church, therefore, must have been a moving spectacle for compassionate minds.

48
00:04:36,239 --> 00:04:41,679
Yet, what I suffered outside was nothing to what I underwent within. The terrors that had assailed

49
00:04:41,679 --> 00:04:47,400
me whenever Mrs. Joe had gone near the pantry, or out of the room, were only to be equalled by the

50
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:54,079
remorse with which my mind dwelt on what my hands had done. Under the weight of my wicked secret,

51
00:04:54,079 --> 00:04:58,199
I pondered whether the Church would be powerful enough to shield me from the vengeance of the

52
00:04:58,199 --> 00:05:04,000
terrible young man, if I divulged to that establishment. I conceived the idea that

53
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,639
the time when the banns were read and when the clergyman said, “Ye are now to declare it!” would

54
00:05:09,639 --> 00:05:16,280
be the time for me to rise and propose a private conference in the vestry. I am far from being

55
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:21,760
sure that I might not have astonished our small congregation by resorting to this extreme measure,

56
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:29,239
but for its being Christmas Day and no Sunday. Mr. Wopsle, the clerk at church, was to dine with

57
00:05:29,239 --> 00:05:35,760
us; and Mr. Hubble the wheelwright and Mrs. Hubble; and Uncle Pumblechook (Joe’s uncle,

58
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:40,880
but Mrs. Joe appropriated him), who was a well-to-do cornchandler in the nearest town,

59
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:47,840
and drove his own chaise-cart. The dinner hour was half-past one. When Joe and I got home,

60
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:53,639
we found the table laid, and Mrs. Joe dressed, and the dinner dressing, and the front door

61
00:05:53,639 --> 00:06:00,119
unlocked (it never was at any other time) for the company to enter by, and everything most

62
00:06:00,119 --> 00:06:07,559
splendid. And still, not a word of the robbery. The time came, without bringing with it any relief

63
00:06:07,559 --> 00:06:15,719
to my feelings, and the company came. Mr. Wopsle, united to a Roman nose and a large shining bald

64
00:06:15,719 --> 00:06:21,880
forehead, had a deep voice which he was uncommonly proud of; indeed it was understood among his

65
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:27,199
acquaintance that if you could only give him his head, he would read the clergyman into fits;

66
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:32,760
he himself confessed that if the Church was “thrown open,” meaning to competition,

67
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:39,199
he would not despair of making his mark in it. The Church not being “thrown open,” he was,

68
00:06:39,199 --> 00:06:45,679
as I have said, our clerk. But he punished the Amens tremendously; and when he gave out the

69
00:06:45,679 --> 00:06:52,039
psalm,—always giving the whole verse,—he looked all round the congregation first, as much as

70
00:06:52,039 --> 00:06:59,480
to say, “You have heard my friend overhead; oblige me with your opinion of this style!”

71
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:04,119
I opened the door to the company,—making believe that it was a habit of ours to open

72
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:11,920
that door,—and I opened it first to Mr. Wopsle, next to Mr. and Mrs. Hubble, and last of all to

73
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:19,519
Uncle Pumblechook. N.B. I was not allowed to call him uncle, under the severest penalties.

74
00:07:19,519 --> 00:07:25,039
“Mrs. Joe,” said Uncle Pumblechook, a large hard-breathing middle-aged slow man,

75
00:07:25,039 --> 00:07:31,239
with a mouth like a fish, dull staring eyes, and sandy hair standing upright on his head,

76
00:07:31,239 --> 00:07:36,280
so that he looked as if he had just been all but choked, and had that moment come to,

77
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:39,679
“I have brought you as the compliments of the season—I have brought you,

78
00:07:39,679 --> 00:07:46,559
Mum, a bottle of sherry wine—and I have brought you, Mum, a bottle of port wine.”

79
00:07:46,559 --> 00:07:52,440
Every Christmas Day he presented himself, as a profound novelty, with exactly the same words,

80
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,639
and carrying the two bottles like dumb-bells.

81
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:04,880
Every Christmas Day, Mrs. Joe replied, as she now replied, “O, Un—cle Pum-ble—chook! This is

82
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:12,239
kind!” Every Christmas Day, he retorted, as he now retorted, “It’s no more than your merits.

83
00:08:12,239 --> 00:08:18,559
And now are you all bobbish, and how’s Sixpennorth of halfpence?” meaning me.

84
00:08:18,559 --> 00:08:23,599
We dined on these occasions in the kitchen, and adjourned, for the nuts and oranges and

85
00:08:23,599 --> 00:08:29,039
apples to the parlour; which was a change very like Joe’s change from his working-clothes to

86
00:08:29,039 --> 00:08:36,159
his Sunday dress. My sister was uncommonly lively on the present occasion, and indeed was generally

87
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:43,360
more gracious in the society of Mrs. Hubble than in other company. I remember Mrs. Hubble as a

88
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:49,119
little curly sharp-edged person in sky-blue, who held a conventionally juvenile position,

89
00:08:49,119 --> 00:08:54,199
because she had married Mr. Hubble,—I don’t know at what remote period,—when she was much

90
00:08:54,199 --> 00:09:01,079
younger than he. I remember Mr Hubble as a tough, high-shouldered, stooping old man,

91
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:05,920
of a sawdusty fragrance, with his legs extraordinarily wide apart: so that in my

92
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:12,280
short days I always saw some miles of open country between them when I met him coming up the lane.

93
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:16,719
Among this good company I should have felt myself, even if I hadn’t robbed the pantry,

94
00:09:16,719 --> 00:09:22,480
in a false position. Not because I was squeezed in at an acute angle of the tablecloth,

95
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:28,559
with the table in my chest, and the Pumblechookian elbow in my eye, nor because I was not allowed to

96
00:09:28,559 --> 00:09:34,960
speak (I didn’t want to speak), nor because I was regaled with the scaly tips of the drumsticks of

97
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:41,880
the fowls, and with those obscure corners of pork of which the pig, when living, had had the least

98
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:49,599
reason to be vain. No; I should not have minded that, if they would only have left me alone. But

99
00:09:49,599 --> 00:09:55,119
they wouldn’t leave me alone. They seemed to think the opportunity lost, if they failed to

100
00:09:55,119 --> 00:10:01,440
point the conversation at me, every now and then, and stick the point into me. I might have been an

101
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:08,320
unfortunate little bull in a Spanish arena, I got so smartingly touched up by these moral goads.

102
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:13,159
It began the moment we sat down to dinner. Mr. Wopsle said grace with

103
00:10:13,159 --> 00:10:18,800
theatrical declamation,—as it now appears to me, something like a religious cross

104
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:24,599
of the Ghost in Hamlet with Richard the Third,—and ended with the very proper

105
00:10:24,599 --> 00:10:31,000
aspiration that we might be truly grateful. Upon which my sister fixed me with her eye,

106
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:38,039
and said, in a low reproachful voice, “Do you hear that? Be grateful.”

107
00:10:38,039 --> 00:10:46,000
“Especially,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “be grateful, boy, to them which brought you up by hand.”

108
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,719
Mrs. Hubble shook her head, and contemplating me with a mournful presentiment that I should

109
00:10:50,719 --> 00:10:57,280
come to no good, asked, “Why is it that the young are never grateful?” This moral mystery

110
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:04,239
seemed too much for the company until Mr. Hubble tersely solved it by saying, “Naterally wicious.”

111
00:11:04,239 --> 00:11:11,079
Everybody then murmured “True!” and looked at me in a particularly unpleasant and personal manner.

112
00:11:11,079 --> 00:11:16,239
Joe’s station and influence were something feebler (if possible) when there was company

113
00:11:16,239 --> 00:11:21,119
than when there was none. But he always aided and comforted me when he could,

114
00:11:21,119 --> 00:11:27,800
in some way of his own, and he always did so at dinner-time by giving me gravy, if there were any.

115
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:35,039
There being plenty of gravy to-day, Joe spooned into my plate, at this point, about half a pint.

116
00:11:35,039 --> 00:11:39,960
A little later on in the dinner, Mr. Wopsle reviewed the sermon with some severity,

117
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:45,760
and intimated—in the usual hypothetical case of the Church being “thrown open”—what kind of sermon

118
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:51,039
he would have given them. After favouring them with some heads of that discourse,

119
00:11:51,039 --> 00:11:55,440
he remarked that he considered the subject of the day’s homily, ill chosen;

120
00:11:55,440 --> 00:12:01,159
which was the less excusable, he added, when there were so many subjects “going about.”

121
00:12:01,159 --> 00:12:08,199
“True again,” said Uncle Pumblechook. “You’ve hit it, sir! Plenty of subjects going about, for them

122
00:12:08,199 --> 00:12:14,280
that know how to put salt upon their tails. That’s what’s wanted. A man needn’t go far to

123
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:20,119
find a subject, if he’s ready with his salt-box.” Mr. Pumblechook added, after a short interval

124
00:12:20,119 --> 00:12:28,639
of reflection, “Look at Pork alone. There’s a subject! If you want a subject, look at Pork!”

125
00:12:28,639 --> 00:12:32,880
“True, sir. Many a moral for the young,” returned Mr. Wopsle,—and

126
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:39,440
I knew he was going to lug me in, before he said it; “might be deduced from that text.”

127
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:44,599
(“You listen to this,” said my sister to me, in a severe parenthesis.)

128
00:12:44,599 --> 00:12:47,320
Joe gave me some more gravy.

129
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:53,159
“Swine,” pursued Mr. Wopsle, in his deepest voice, and pointing his fork at my blushes,

130
00:12:53,159 --> 00:12:57,480
as if he were mentioning my Christian name,—“swine were

131
00:12:57,480 --> 00:13:03,440
the companions of the prodigal. The gluttony of Swine is put before us,

132
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:08,840
as an example to the young.” (I thought this pretty well in him who had been praising up

133
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:16,239
the pork for being so plump and juicy.) “What is detestable in a pig is more detestable in a boy.”

134
00:13:16,239 --> 00:13:19,480
“Or girl,” suggested Mr. Hubble.

135
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:24,599
“Of course, or girl, Mr. Hubble,” assented Mr. Wopsle,

136
00:13:24,599 --> 00:13:28,800
rather irritably, “but there is no girl present.”

137
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:32,639
“Besides,” said Mr. Pumblechook, turning sharp on me, “think what

138
00:13:32,639 --> 00:13:36,280
you’ve got to be grateful for. If you’d been born a Squeaker—”

139
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:41,239
“He was, if ever a child was,” said my sister, most emphatically.

140
00:13:41,239 --> 00:13:43,440
Joe gave me some more gravy.

141
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:47,280
“Well, but I mean a four-footed Squeaker,” said Mr. Pumblechook.

142
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:51,880
“If you had been born such, would you have been here now? Not you—”

143
00:13:51,880 --> 00:13:56,480
“Unless in that form,” said Mr. Wopsle, nodding towards the dish.

144
00:13:56,480 --> 00:14:01,400
“But I don’t mean in that form, sir,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, who had an objection to being

145
00:14:01,400 --> 00:14:06,760
interrupted; “I mean, enjoying himself with his elders and betters, and improving himself

146
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:12,320
with their conversation, and rolling in the lap of luxury. Would he have been doing that? No,

147
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:18,280
he wouldn’t. And what would have been your destination?” turning on me again. “You would

148
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:22,800
have been disposed of for so many shillings according to the market price of the article,

149
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:26,480
and Dunstable the butcher would have come up to you as you lay in your straw,

150
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:30,559
and he would have whipped you under his left arm, and with his right he would have tucked

151
00:14:30,559 --> 00:14:34,440
up his frock to get a penknife from out of his waistcoat-pocket, and he would

152
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:39,800
have shed your blood and had your life. No bringing up by hand then. Not a bit of it!”

153
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:43,320
Joe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to take.

154
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:48,760
“He was a world of trouble to you, ma’am,” said Mrs. Hubble, commiserating my sister.

155
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:54,480
“Trouble?” echoed my sister; “trouble?” and then entered on a fearful catalogue

156
00:14:54,480 --> 00:15:00,159
of all the illnesses I had been guilty of, and all the acts of sleeplessness I had committed,

157
00:15:00,159 --> 00:15:05,079
and all the high places I had tumbled from, and all the low places I had tumbled into,

158
00:15:05,079 --> 00:15:09,559
and all the injuries I had done myself, and all the times she had wished me in my grave,

159
00:15:09,559 --> 00:15:13,000
and I had contumaciously refused to go there.

160
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:18,159
I think the Romans must have aggravated one another very much, with their noses. Perhaps,

161
00:15:18,159 --> 00:15:22,960
they became the restless people they were, in consequence. Anyhow,

162
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:27,920
Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose so aggravated me, during the recital of my misdemeanours,

163
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:32,039
that I should have liked to pull it until he howled. But, all I had endured up to

164
00:15:32,039 --> 00:15:36,079
this time was nothing in comparison with the awful feelings that took possession

165
00:15:36,079 --> 00:15:42,280
of me when the pause was broken which ensued upon my sister’s recital, and in which pause

166
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:49,320
everybody had looked at me (as I felt painfully conscious) with indignation and abhorrence.

167
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:54,159
“Yet,” said Mr. Pumblechook, leading the company gently back to the theme from which

168
00:15:54,159 --> 00:16:01,239
they had strayed, “Pork—regarded as biled—is rich, too; ain’t it?”

169
00:16:01,239 --> 00:16:04,679
“Have a little brandy, uncle,” said my sister.

170
00:16:04,679 --> 00:16:10,320
O Heavens, it had come at last! He would find it was weak, he would say it was weak, and I was

171
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:19,239
lost! I held tight to the leg of the table under the cloth, with both hands, and awaited my fate.

172
00:16:19,239 --> 00:16:25,400
My sister went for the stone bottle, came back with the stone bottle, and poured his brandy out:

173
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:32,599
no one else taking any. The wretched man trifled with his glass,—took it up, looked at it through

174
00:16:32,599 --> 00:16:39,960
the light, put it down,—prolonged my misery. All this time Mrs. Joe and Joe were briskly

175
00:16:39,960 --> 00:16:45,960
clearing the table for the pie and pudding. I couldn’t keep my eyes off him. Always

176
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:50,920
holding tight by the leg of the table with my hands and feet, I saw the miserable creature

177
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:58,320
finger his glass playfully, take it up, smile, throw his head back, and drink the brandy off.

178
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:04,320
Instantly afterwards, the company were seized with unspeakable consternation, owing to his springing

179
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:10,079
to his feet, turning round several times in an appalling spasmodic whooping-cough dance,

180
00:17:10,079 --> 00:17:15,519
and rushing out at the door; he then became visible through the window, violently plunging

181
00:17:15,519 --> 00:17:22,239
and expectorating, making the most hideous faces, and apparently out of his mind.

182
00:17:22,239 --> 00:17:28,320
I held on tight, while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him. I didn’t know how I had done it,

183
00:17:28,320 --> 00:17:33,719
but I had no doubt I had murdered him somehow. In my dreadful situation,

184
00:17:33,719 --> 00:17:39,400
it was a relief when he was brought back, and surveying the company all round as if they

185
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:46,920
had disagreed with him, sank down into his chair with the one significant gasp, “Tar!”

186
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:53,519
I had filled up the bottle from the tar-water jug. I knew he would be worse by and by. I

187
00:17:53,519 --> 00:18:00,840
moved the table, like a Medium of the present day, by the vigor of my unseen hold upon it.

188
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:06,800
“Tar!” cried my sister, in amazement. “Why, how ever could Tar come there?”

189
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:12,079
But, Uncle Pumblechook, who was omnipotent in that kitchen, wouldn’t hear the word, wouldn’t

190
00:18:12,079 --> 00:18:19,199
hear of the subject, imperiously waved it all away with his hand, and asked for hot gin and water. My

191
00:18:19,199 --> 00:18:25,800
sister, who had begun to be alarmingly meditative, had to employ herself actively in getting the gin,

192
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:32,199
the hot water, the sugar, and the lemon-peel, and mixing them. For the time being at least, I was

193
00:18:32,199 --> 00:18:39,679
saved. I still held on to the leg of the table, but clutched it now with the fervor of gratitude.

194
00:18:39,679 --> 00:18:43,679
By degrees, I became calm enough to release my grasp and partake of

195
00:18:43,679 --> 00:18:51,960
pudding. Mr. Pumblechook partook of pudding. All partook of pudding. The course terminated,

196
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:58,119
and Mr. Pumblechook had begun to beam under the genial influence of gin and water. I began to

197
00:18:58,119 --> 00:19:04,000
think I should get over the day, when my sister said to Joe, “Clean plates,—cold.”

198
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,159
I clutched the leg of the table again immediately,

199
00:19:07,159 --> 00:19:12,159
and pressed it to my bosom as if it had been the companion of my youth and friend of my

200
00:19:12,159 --> 00:19:19,199
soul. I foresaw what was coming, and I felt that this time I really was gone.

201
00:19:19,199 --> 00:19:24,280
“You must taste,” said my sister, addressing the guests with her best grace—“you

202
00:19:24,280 --> 00:19:29,960
must taste, to finish with, such a delightful and delicious present of Uncle Pumblechook’s!”

203
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:33,840
Must they! Let them not hope to taste it!

204
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:39,360
“You must know,” said my sister, rising, “it’s a pie; a savory pork pie.”

205
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:44,159
The company murmured their compliments. Uncle Pumblechook, sensible of having deserved

206
00:19:44,159 --> 00:19:48,920
well of his fellow-creatures, said,—quite vivaciously, all things considered,—“Well,

207
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:54,280
Mrs. Joe, we’ll do our best endeavours; let us have a cut at this same pie.”

208
00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:59,000
My sister went out to get it. I heard her steps proceed to the pantry. I saw

209
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Mr. Pumblechook balance his knife. I saw reawakening appetite in the Roman nostrils

210
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:09,480
of Mr. Wopsle. I heard Mr. Hubble remark that “a bit of savory pork pie would lay atop of

211
00:20:09,480 --> 00:20:15,320
anything you could mention, and do no harm,” and I heard Joe say, “You shall have some,

212
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:20,920
Pip.” I have never been absolutely certain whether I uttered a shrill yell of terror,

213
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:27,000
merely in spirit, or in the bodily hearing of the company. I felt that I could bear no more,

214
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:33,320
and that I must run away. I released the leg of the table, and ran for my life.

215
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:38,719
But I ran no farther than the house door, for there I ran head-foremost into a party of

216
00:20:38,719 --> 00:20:45,360
soldiers with their muskets, one of whom held out a pair of handcuffs to me, saying, “Here you are,

217
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:48,000
look sharp, come on!” Chapter 5.
